WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.920
When we prepared the petition for the federal court, and Reverend Jemison did not want that.

2
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.220
Why?

3
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:37.580
Because he said he wanted the power structure of this city and of this parish to know that we still . . . that the black people still had faith in them and trust them to do right.

4
00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:42.700
He was a Christian, you know, that’s religious talk.

5
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.540
That was alright in the church house, but that doesn’t mean a thing in the court house.

6
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:48.860
You see?

7
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:54.740
I don’t know if . . . It’s hard to get Martin Luther King, one of the few preachers,

8
00:00:54.740 --> 00:01:09.740
and another guy Stalworth I think his name was, out in Alabama, they were one of the few people that saw that difference.

9
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.960
That the church house was one thing; the court house was another.

10
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:20.020
Do you think the bus boycott may have been just too soon for Baton Rouge to handle?

11
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:35.860
The time had not quite come for Jemison do what Dr. Martin Luther King did, but he did a yeoman’s job, a very good job.

12
00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.560
He start the ball a-rolling.

13
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.440
He got things started.

14
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.760
He opened our eyes.

15
00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:51.940
Martin Luther King sought out Jemison to get information on how to proceed with a boycott.

16
00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.540
He was a forerunner.

17
00:01:53.540 --> 00:02:01.560
And he . . . His philosophy was filled with good intentions.

18
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:09.700
“Let them see that we still believe that they will do right.

19
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.600
We trust them.”

20
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.360
And he was hoping.

21
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:26.740
He was hoping that they would go ahead on and desegregate the buses in a different manner.

22
00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:38.860
But this with from front to back was what he thought was a step forward.

23
00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:47.960
Which it was because you didn’t have to . . . everybody wouldn’t . . . everybody had to stand up at one time or another.

24
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:53.680
And the other one couldn’t tell the other one to move unless the other one being in violation of the law.

25
00:02:53.680 --> 00:03:01.640
So it put . . . It put everybody in a position to be in violation of the law.

26
00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:16.520
Whereas before Ordinance 222, blacks were always, every time, anytime in violation of the law.

27
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.220
If you want to make me so.
